% =!=> adds a hard cut to the tail of a hypothetical rule, so a `func`
% predicate stays provably deterministic even when a rule is added for it
% at run time. With plain ==> instead, this raises "mutual exclusion
% violated": `copy a b` and the static `copy X X` could both apply to
% `copy a Z`.

kind tm type.
symbol a : tm.
symbol b : tm.

func copy tm -> tm.
copy X X.

main :- copy a b =!=> copy a Z, print "copy a gives" Z.
